<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<archimate:model xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:archimate="http://www.archimatetool.com/archimate" name="WHO Models" id="id-e3a716cdc31042b0a745fec8e4e7a73c" version="5.0.0">
  <folder name="Strategy" id="id-37e6f7416def4399a1ef2a00e089ddb5" type="strategy">
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Workforce Education and Training" id="id-a57861c106b34362a06b8a19d17445bc"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Health Workforce Management" id="id-2fa68d5b309d4464863acefb4973f14b">
      <documentation>The organizational capability to strategically plan, deploy, and monitor the entire healthcare staff across various shifts and departments. It encompasses everything from scheduling and credentials tracking to ensuring the right clinical skills are available where they are needed most.&#xD;
&#xD;
It optimizes labor costs, prevents employee burnout, and guarantees that the hospital remains adequately staffed to provide safe patient care. Additionally, it ensures that all hours worked are accurately recorded and aligned with labor laws for seamless payroll processing.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: &quot;The hospital's centralized system for managing nurse rotations, tracking physician on-call schedules, and monitoring temporary staffing needs across all clinical wards.&quot;</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Privacy / Consent/ Trust" id="id-10b2e5822bf6480aaf10d28e42e2cb69">
      <documentation>The core operational capability to enforce data confidentiality, capture and respect individual data-sharing preferences, and maintain absolute security across all digital interactions. This capability ensures that a user's personal information—whether patient medical records or employee financial data—is processed strictly in accordance with legal mandates and explicit permissions.&#xD;
&#xD;
Possessing this capability eliminates the risk of unauthorized data leaks, regulatory non-compliance fines, and systemic breaches that damage institutional reputation. For leadership, it creates a secure foundation that encourages widespread user adoption, ensuring that citizens and staff confidently trust digital platforms with their most sensitive personal and professional data.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: &quot;The Privacy, Consent, and Trust capability that automatically applies a patient's digital consent directives across the network, instantly blocking unauthorized clinics from accessing their medical history without explicit, time-bound permission.&quot;</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Client Identity Management" id="id-438335f9e552485399e2e7d6f9864b1c">
      <documentation>The core operational ability to capture, store, update, and authenticate the unique digital profiles of individual patients or service recipients across a healthcare ecosystem. This capability ensures that a client's demographic data, consent preferences, and system identifiers are managed securely and consistently at every point of care.&#xD;
&#xD;
Possessing this capability eliminates the risks of duplicate medical records, misidentification, and unauthorized data exposure, directly protecting patient safety. For administrative and clinical workflows, it provides a trusted foundation for matching the right treatment, billing records, and clinical history to the correct individual, regardless of which facility they visit.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: &quot;The enterprise Client Identity Management capability that enables a regional hospital network to instantly resolve cross-facility patient records and securely verify a patient's identity using a national health card.&quot;</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Interoperability Management" id="id-67c933eb87a140d7acda3fd84616f62e">
      <documentation>The core operational capability to design, enforce, and maintain secure data exchanges and seamless communication between disparate digital systems across an enterprise or national infrastructure. This capability relies on the strict adoption of global data standards (such as FHIR for health data or ISO standards for finance) and structured API architectures to ensure that different software platforms can read, interpret, and act upon shared information without human intervention.&#xD;
&#xD;
Possessing this capability eliminates the fragmentation of data silos, manual double-entry of records, and the high maintenance costs of building custom, brittle point-to-point connections. For leadership, it provides a highly flexible and scalable digital ecosystem where new software modules, regional databases, and third-party tools can be integrated quickly and cost-effectively, maintaining an uncompromised flow of truth across the entire network.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: &quot;The Interoperability Management capability that allows a regional clinic's open-source record system to instantly pull and understand a doctor's credential status from the National Health Workforce Registry via standard RESTful APIs.&quot;</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Health Financing Management" id="id-7ee19138449445078bee19c840d8fc7f">
      <documentation>To strategically mobilize, allocate, pool, and distribute financial resources across a healthcare ecosystem to ensure sustainable funding, equitable provider reimbursement, and maximized financial protection for patients. This capability handles the complex backend processes of tracking national health budgets, managing insurance premium pools, processing provider claims, and purchasing clinical services efficiently.&#xD;
&#xD;
Possessing this capability eliminates financial fragmentation, leakages, and delayed payments, ensuring that healthcare funds are spent efficiently and transparently. For leadership, it provides the precise fiscal data needed to optimize purchasing strategies, adjust provider payment models, and sustainably scale universal health coverage programs without risking institutional bankruptcy.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: &quot;The national Health Financing Management capability that enabled the automated processing and verification of 50,000 public insurance claims daily, cutting provider reimbursement cycles from six months down to 48 hours.&quot;</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Analytics &amp; Business Intelligence" id="id-f7ca430bf2fd496094fb68da53f6eb00">
      <documentation>The organizational capability to collect, analyze, and visualize complex operational and financial data from various hospital departments. It translates vast amounts of raw data into user-friendly dashboards and reports that give leadership a clear view of organizational performance.&#xD;
&#xD;
It empowers management to make proactive, evidence-based decisions that improve efficiency and identify cost-saving opportunities. By spotting trends early, the hospital can quickly adapt to workforce changes, budget constraints, and regulatory requirements.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: &quot;The deployment of interactive dashboards that allow senior hospital executives to track real-time labor expenses, monitor overtime patterns, and forecast future staffing costs.&quot;</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Governance, Policy, and Legislation" id="id-d645f6d3e4bd4596aa959a9c817648ca">
      <documentation>The strategic operational capability to establish, enforce, and adapt the legal frameworks, structural rules, and compliance standards that oversee a digital ecosystem. This capability ensures that all institutional strategies, digital platforms, and data exchanges are explicitly backed by binding legislation, clear accountability structures, and formalized organizational policies.&#xD;
&#xD;
Possessing this capability eliminates regulatory ambiguity, mitigates legal risks, and builds public and institutional trust in digital systems. For leadership, it provides the mandatory mandate and oversight required to enforce data privacy compliance, secure long-term public funding, and align multi-sector stakeholders under a unified digital roadmap.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: &quot;The national Governance, Policy, and Legislation capability that enabled the drafting and passage of the Digital Health Act, legally enforcing the adoption of standard registries and securing the statutory budget for national data infrastructure.&quot;</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Production (Pre-Service Education)" id="id-8d74939be8374ac0b783567d362b7c6a"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Value Stream" id="id-3ca1207a3038499482f7610050f0189a"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Entry into Workforce (Employed )" id="id-ca900f2ee57741f8a26561c90c2970bb"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Maintenance &amp; Performance (Optimizing)" id="id-75e26f5282c348b5b90d9231ba53ea61"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Exit from Workforce" id="id-8a3042d52a1d45ac929156edd4f1dc66"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Risk Assessment &amp; Intelligence" id="id-78753f1b5c4d449e9b2ea73c4ee9db9f"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Data Governance &amp; Privacy" id="id-7ea585fe5d154d91996cec080eebc63a"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Data Sharing and Interoperability" id="id-1766275e8ce04871a5fcee90208f1275"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Report Priority Diseases, Conditions and Events" id="id-76bde7e985954252b18df7200d1d40f3"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Detect and Record Cases of Priority Diseases, Conditions and Events" id="id-f9ccbb87181a46fc9a5f7466657797d5"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Analyze Data" id="id-beac47d5790144f6bf277a688ea4451a"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Analyze data from reporting sites" id="id-0dc5ef4902e14e419faec0a9efe1743b"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Utilize Analysis to Improve Public Health Action" id="id-e7ad8cfd662c44818958dd1757a7c632"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Surveillance Capabilities" id="id-d5b7178ecef64a24af9d8b4b446268ca"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Event Detection and Case Notification" id="id-d9ebddc14ce141d2b9c37d7b30fdc923"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Adverse Event Reporting and Safety Signals" id="id-59de8f4dfe18475abf96e78d11a0fdfb"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Coordinated Response Management" id="id-e42f51d04bb74e4888de44ec02644128"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Knowledge Dissemination and Alerts" id="id-3a54623a2e2a4cfb867c090bcafdc4e6"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Health Record Management" id="id-ea7e47ce0db44837bf267dab146eaaae">
      <documentation>This capability involves the lifecycle management of longitudinal patient records, ensuring they are verifiable, accurate, and accessible over time. It encompasses the capture, storage, and retrieval of clinical data in a standardized format.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to maintain a continuous and trustworthy medical history for every individual throughout their life. It provides the foundational data layer required for integrated care and patient empowerment.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: A system capability to link a patient’s childhood immunization record with their current health status 20 years later.&#xD;
</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Identity &amp; Authentication" id="id-d451aeba549a49498c5ba8866dd3ef32">
      <documentation>This capability provides the technical means to uniquely identify citizens and verify their identity before allowing access to records. It often links a health-specific ID to a national registry or digital identity provider.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to ensure that health records are linked to the correct person and that unauthorized access is prevented. This is a critical security layer for protecting patient privacy and ensuring data integrity.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: A biometric-based login service that authenticates a patient before displaying their medical results.&#xD;
</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Universal Access Enablement" id="id-1769b95a965c44798e9ae59acb08b29f">
      <documentation>This capability ensures that personal health services and records are accessible to all citizens through multiple channels, including web portals and mobile devices. It focuses on inclusivity and reducing barriers to health information access.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to drive widespread adoption and ensure that all individuals, regardless of their location or device, can benefit from their health data. This supports the goal of equitable access to modern health services.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: A multi-channel PHR portal that works on both high-end smartphones and basic feature phones via USSD.&#xD;
</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Dynamic Consent Management" id="id-2fd76aa097664e5b8dd80d7c4be06eb6">
      <documentation>This capability allows for the granular and real-time management of patient permissions for data sharing. It enables patients to grant, revoke, or modify access rights for different providers and purposes.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to place the patient in control of their data and ensure that sharing is always based on explicit, informed consent. This is a key requirement for building trust and complying with privacy laws.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: A &quot;consent dashboard&quot; where a patient can give a specialist access to their history for only a 24-hour window.&#xD;
</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Data Governance" id="id-b3c63fae4d0044bab340bc9d05caeb2c">
      <documentation>This capability establishes the policies, standards, and rules for the quality, security, and ethical use of health data. It involves the management of metadata, data provenance, and stewardship responsibilities.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to ensure that data within the ecosystem is reliable, standardized, and used appropriately. It provides the framework for accountability and trust across all participating stakeholders.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: A national data council defining the mandatory data quality standards for all PHR-contributing facilities.&#xD;
</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Data Sharing &amp; Authorization" id="id-0284a80889b14adc8ad08094830c4ca0">
      <documentation>This capability handles the technical processes of exchanging data between systems based on authorized requests and valid consent. It ensures that data flows securely and only to parties who have the legal right to see it.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to enable seamless interoperability while strictly enforcing access control policies. This allows for clinical data to follow the patient across different levels of care without compromising privacy.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: An authorization server issuing temporary tokens to allow a lab system to push results to a patient’s PHR.&#xD;
</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Audit &amp; Compliance Monitoring" id="id-40dba44e3b16421b9b2aff511b1a4598">
      <documentation>This capability involves the continuous tracking and recording of all data access and system activities for oversight and accountability. It provides the audit trails necessary to verify that all transactions comply with established policies.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to provide transparency and ensure that any unauthorized or improper use of data is detected and addressed. This supports regulatory compliance and reinforces stakeholder trust in system integrity.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: A monitoring service that alerts administrators if a patient record is accessed without a corresponding consent record.&#xD;
</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Trust &amp; Verification" id="id-475f859cfb54420b8b473aa67489ca4c">
      <documentation>This capability uses cryptographic services to verify the source, authenticity, and integrity of digital health records and transactions. It often involves the use of digital certificates and signatures.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to establish technical trust in the &#xD;
absence of a central authority for every transaction. It ensures that any recipient can prove a record is authentic and has not been tampered with.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: A verification service that checks the digital signature on an e-prescription before it is filled by a pharmacy.&#xD;
</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Data Privacy" id="id-e8a27bc7c10b45dbadf0267996047e44">
      <documentation>This capability encompasses the technical and administrative controls used to protect sensitive data from unauthorized disclosure or breach. It includes encryption, data masking, and secure storage protocols.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to protect the privacy of individuals and safeguard health information against cyber threats. It is a fundamental requirement for maintaining public confidence in digital health initiatives.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: Implementing end-to-end encryption for all patient data stored in the national PHR repository.&#xD;
</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Incident Detection &amp; Response" id="id-8b67470531fb4599ac267b05edbf7357">
      <documentation>This capability involves the proactive monitoring of the health ecosystem for cybersecurity incidents and the execution of response plans when breaches occur. It aims to minimize the impact of security events on system operations and data privacy.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to ensure the resilience of the health infrastructure and provide a rapid recovery mechanism after a security event. This protects the continuity of care and the integrity of the health data network.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: A Security Operations Center (SOC) that detects and blocks a brute-force attack on the PHR login portal.&#xD;
</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Interoperability &amp; Health Information Exchange" id="id-01f2b2b340fb4d65acd156dd621e1898">
      <documentation>This capability provides the technical infrastructure and standards required for different health IT systems to talk to each other and share data. It involves the use of common APIs, messaging standards, and exchange protocols.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to enable a unified patient view by connecting fragmented health data sources across the entire health system. This is the primary driver for clinical coordination and longitudinal record creation.&#xD;
Example: A national HIE hub that routes clinical &#xD;
summaries between public clinics and private specialist centers.&#xD;
</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Termininology &amp; Data Management" id="id-21e683fe8e134733aa04bb805322f327">
      <documentation>This capability focuses on the standardization of clinical languages and data formats, such as SNOMED CT, ICD-11, and LOINC. It ensures that the meaning of health data is preserved during exchange.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to achieve semantic interoperability, where systems can accurately interpret and act upon the data they receive. This is essential for clinical decision support, analytics, and reporting.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: A central terminology server that maps local hospital &quot;chest pain&quot; codes to the international ICD-11 standard.&#xD;
</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Provider &amp; Facility Verification" id="id-f37b40d81de9487baec94b35445ed8e9">
      <documentation>This capability involves the management and verification of the credentials, licenses, and operational status of health workers and facilities. It ensures that only legitimate and qualified parties participate in the network.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to establish trust in the identities of the parties providing and accessing health information. This prevents unauthorized practice and ensures that patient data is only handled by verified professionals.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: An automated check against the national health worker registry before a doctor is allowed to sign a medical report.&#xD;
</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Financial Planning &amp; Budgeting" id="id-1063e86fbba34ac499533e39a3ed620b">
      <documentation>This value stream involves the structured process of estimating health expenditures and allocating financial resources based on projected population needs. It integrates historical spending data with predictive modeling to create sustainable fiscal plans for the health sector.&#xD;
&#xD;
The objective is to ensure that health programs have reliable funding and that resources are distributed efficiently to prevent budget shortfalls. It enables decision-makers to align financial commitments with long-term strategic health goals.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: A rolling 12-month budget cycle that adjusts funding for maternal health services based on real-time facility usage reports.</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Strategic Purchasing &amp; Provider Payment" id="id-7ff62c4f0291485cbb122e29f0d8da2f">
      <documentation>This value stream manages the active procurement of healthcare services from providers using evidence-based contracts and standardized payment models. It transitions health financing from passive budget transfers to result-oriented reimbursement cycles.&#xD;
&#xD;
The goal is to maximize the value of health spending by incentivizing high-quality care and improving clinical efficiency. It ensures that healthcare providers are paid fairly and promptly according to established performance metrics and tariffs.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: A social health fund reimbursing hospitals based on &quot;cost-per-case&quot; bundles rather than fixed annual grants.</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Member Protection &amp; Entitlement Management" id="id-71edfe145d0b4330915f853c28e4498e">
      <documentation>This value stream focuses on enrolling individuals into health insurance schemes and managing their specific benefit entitlements. It identifies eligible populations and ensures they are protected from financial hardship caused by medical costs.&#xD;
&#xD;
The intent is to provide universal financial protection and ensure citizens can access their covered health benefits at the point of care. It reduces out-of-pocket spending and safeguards individuals from catastrophic health-related debts.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: An automated system that verifies a citizen's insurance coverage and applies the correct subsidy during a hospital visit.</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Transparency &amp; Accountability" id="id-b7d09b24e45549f19500f3ac46d45cd8"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Financial Data Exchange &amp; Reconciliation" id="id-9e98c8a7da4749d2864bbd2a82268e83"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Personal Health Records Creation" id="id-da7a3d4681b24ebc866989dd95a03b08">
      <documentation>This value stream encompasses the initial registration of an individual and the setup of their foundational digital profile. It involves capturing baseline demographics and linking them to a unique, verifiable health identifier.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to provide every person with a trusted entry point into the digital health ecosystem. It establishes the necessary structure for building a lifelong, longitudinal record that follows the patient across all healthcare facilities.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: A community health worker registering a citizen and issuing a digital health ID that links to the central Client Registry.</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Health Record Management &amp; Maintenance" id="id-e988d4d01b004d10b9af4bc234039272">
      <documentation>This value stream manages the ongoing updates, versioning, and consolidation of a patient's clinical and demographic data. It ensures that fragmented information from different sources is correctly matched and integrated into the primary record.&#xD;
&#xD;
The aim is to maintain the accuracy and clinical integrity of the health record throughout an individual's lifetime. Regular maintenance prevents data duplication and ensures that providers have access to the most current and reliable information.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: The system automatically merging lab results from two different clinics into a single chronological timeline for the patient.</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Personal Health Records Access and Sharing" id="id-a87a8b0e8387443393ea5b87c39971aa">
      <documentation>This value stream focuses on the technical and procedural mechanisms that allow patients and providers to retrieve and exchange health information. It leverages portals and interoperability gateways to facilitate the secure movement of data across the health network.&#xD;
&#xD;
The intent is to enable seamless care coordination and empower patients to govern their own health information. It ensures that medical data is portable and available at the point of care, regardless of where the information was originally generated.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: A citizen granting a pharmacist temporary access to their current medication list through a secure consent-based dashboard.</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Personal Health Records Archiving and Disposal" id="id-20dbcbe1c86a4bfb81c58aa6b5c676bf">
      <documentation>This value stream governs the long-term storage and eventual permanent destruction of health records according to established retention policies. It manages the transition of data from active use to secure archival or final disposal once it is no longer required.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to meet legal record-keeping mandates while protecting patient privacy by securely removing data that has reached the end of its lifecycle. It optimizes system performance and security by ensuring that only relevant and necessary information is maintained in active databases.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: Securely deleting a patient's clinical records twenty years after their death in accordance with national data protection laws.</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Security &amp; Compliance" id="id-ed96f6b41afd408184afd86034b10cc5">
      <documentation>This value stream provides the overarching security framework and technical controls used to protect health data and ensure regulatory adherence. It includes the enforcement of privacy policies, encryption of sensitive information, and continuous auditing of all system interactions.&#xD;
&#xD;
The objective is to safeguard the privacy of individuals and maintain high levels of trust within the digital health ecosystem. It ensures that the system remains resilient against cyber threats and compliant with international data protection standards such as GDPR.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: An automated security service that monitors for unauthorized access attempts and logs all successful clinical record retrievals for audit purposes.</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Health Record Management" id="id-64d4ce834c4d4e14a1c1e4ba95294ff2">
      <documentation>This capability involves the lifecycle management of longitudinal patient records, ensuring they are verifiable, accurate, and accessible over time. It encompasses the capture, storage, and retrieval of clinical data in a standardized format.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to maintain a continuous and trustworthy medical history for every individual throughout their life. It provides the foundational data layer required for integrated care and patient empowerment.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: A system capability to link a patient’s childhood immunization record with their current health status 20 years later.</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Identity &amp; Authentication" id="id-f56db522aa8b452e89c3f3c4a897e871">
      <documentation>This capability provides the technical means to uniquely identify citizens and verify their identity before allowing access to records. It often links a health-specific ID to a national registry or digital identity provider.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to ensure that health records are linked to the correct person and that unauthorized access is prevented. This is a critical security layer for protecting patient privacy and ensuring data integrity.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: A biometric-based login service that authenticates a patient before displaying their medical results.</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Universal Access Enablement" id="id-988039459490443a94db25a0a3b3739a">
      <documentation>This capability ensures that personal health services and records are accessible to all citizens through multiple channels, including web portals and mobile devices. It focuses on inclusivity and reducing barriers to health information access.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to drive widespread adoption and ensure that all individuals, regardless of their location or device, can benefit from their health data. This supports the goal of equitable access to modern health services.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: A multi-channel PHR portal that works on both high-end smartphones and basic feature phones via USSD.&#xD;
</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Financial Safeguards &amp; Risk Management" id="id-42235973c41c404bb12ab719d06320dd"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ValueStream" name="Operational Efficiency &amp; Process Optimization" id="id-073caf12eef64ec4911cbcd7a6ff765b"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="Health Record Management" id="id-5835e70371f14171a8c271e71854ec13">
      <documentation>This capability involves the lifecycle management of longitudinal patient records, ensuring they are verifiable, accurate, and accessible over time. It encompasses the capture, storage, and retrieval of clinical data in a standardized format.&#xD;
